    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d619">

                <pb facs="0914" n="808" sameAs="frus:frus1933-39/pg808"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">361.11 Employees/361: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in the Soviet Union</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Steinhardt</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the
                        Secretary of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Moscow</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1939-12-29T18:00:00+03:00">December 29,
                            1939—6 p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received December 29—3:47 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>1156. Department’s 321, December 28, 6 p.m. As the result of continued pressure
                    the local authorities at Grozny have granted permission for all the American
                    engineers employed by Max B. Miller and Company there to proceed to Moscow for
                    the purpose of validating their passports but after promising transportation now
                    claim that none is presently available. The statement by <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Machinoimport</hi> that the engineers “for unknown causes insist on
                    leaving” is a deliberate evasion in that for nearly a week <hi
                        rendition="#italic">Machinoimport</hi> has known the reason for the proposed
                    trip and <hi rendition="#italic">Grozenft</hi><anchor n="6" xml:id="d619-f1"/>
                    at the instigation of <hi rendition="#italic">Machinoimport</hi> misrepresented
                    to the engineers several days ago that arrangement has been made with the
                    Embassy as a result of which the trip was unnecessary. As <hi
                        rendition="#italic">Machinoimport</hi> has in no way and at no time been in
                    communication with the Embassy this misrepresentation could only have been made
                    for the purpose of misleading the engineers into believing the trip to Moscow to
                    be unnecessary.</p>
                <p>The substance of the Department’s telegraphic instruction under reference was
                    conveyed to Potemkin in my communication of December 25 to which he has not yet
                    replied.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Steinhardt</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d619-f1" n="6" xml:id="d619-f1-content">
                <p>The Grozny oil organization operating the oil fields at Grozny.</p>
            </note>
